 note('Both quotient formulas require dx/dt ≠ 0. Here t ≠ 0; canceling t in the first derivative does not establish the parametric formula at t = 0. The two branches y = 2x − 1 ± (x − 1)³/² have limiting first slope 2 at x = 1, while their second derivative is unbounded there.'),
 heading('4.1.3 MAPLE command diff repeatedly to find higher order derivative of a function'),paragraph('The procedure to use of MAPLE command diff is illustrated in the following example.'),
